ng  it wrong it's spelled saint raphael but  it's pronounced  st raffle well when it was built  glengarry county was the eastern tip of  lower canada  scottish settlers started construction  in 1815  it was completed in 1821 we talked about  the seating there was a thought it would  hold a thousand  that's a lot of people yeah david  anderson is the archivist for the group  that's worked to conserve the site  he says when saint raufels was built  that old road out front was an important  thoroughfare  this was built to be right on the throat  of the great highway of the canadas  between toronto and montreal  stagecoaches ran down that street out  there dundas street  every day now there are theories about  what happened but no one knows for sure  on a warm night 50 years ago this month  someone  passing noticed a glow in an upper  window there was a fire in the roof  they called the fire department right  away eight generations of bernie  mccullough's family  had gone to st raffle this old building  with all them old timbers  there was nothing the fire departments  could do like they came from all over  they came from alexandria williamstown  martin town and all the departments came  but it's not going to win that fight  they're not going to win that it was  something that was that old what had  been so  important for so long 150 years  was reduced to rubble and the insurance  didn't come close to covering the loss  well in the 90s the community banded  together to protect the site  donations and fundraising paid to  stabilize it and in 1999 it was made a  national historic site  this church means so much to me for  dianna sturkenbaum that's meant  protecting the memories of the building  where she was baptized  delivered her speech as valedictorian  and where she was married  57 years ago this week i just love  when i come up here and someone starts  asking questions about the history of  the church and  i just love sharing it with others the  french one  with the 